      "text": "To find the unique two-digit positive integer \\( t \\) such that the last two digits of \\( 11 \\cdot t \\) are 36, we need to solve the congruence:\n\\[\n11t \\equiv 36 \\pmod{100}\n\\]\n\nFirst, we will clear the congruence by finding the multiplicative inverse of 11 modulo 100. The multiplicative inverse \\( x \\) of 11 modulo 100 is a number such that:\n\\[\n11x \\equiv 1 \\pmod{100}\n\\]\n\nWe use the Extended Euclidean Algorithm to find \\( x \\). We start with the Euclidean algorithm to find the greatest common divisor (gcd) of 11 and 100:\n\n\\[\n100 = 11 \\cdot 9 + 1\n\\]\n\\[\n11 = 11 \\cdot 1 + 0\n\\]\n\nThe gcd is 1, and we can express 1 as a combination of 11 and 100:\n\\[\n1 = 100 - 11 \\cdot 9\n\\]\n\nThis equation tells us that:\n\\[\n-11 \\cdot 9 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{100}\n\\]\n\nThus, the multiplicative inverse of 11 modulo 100 is \\(-9\\). Since we need a positive inverse, we convert \\(-9\\) to a positive equivalent by adding 100:\n\\[\n-9 + 100 = 91\n\\]\n\nSo, the multiplicative inverse of 11 modulo 100 is 91. We now multiply both sides of the original congruence \\( 11t \\equiv 36 \\pmod{100} \\) by 91:\n\\[\nt \\equiv 36 \\cdot 91 \\pmod{100}\n\\]\n\nNext, we calculate \\( 36 \\cdot 91 \\):\n\\[\n36 \\cdot 91 = 3276\n\\]\n\nWe reduce 3276 modulo 100:\n\\[\n3276 \\div 100 = 32 \\text{ remainder } 76\n\\]\n\\[\n3276 \\equiv 76 \\pmod{100}\n\\]\n\nThus, we have:\n\\[\nt \\equiv 76 \\pmod{100}\n\\]\n\nSince \\( t \\) is a two-digit positive integer, we conclude:\n\\[\nt = 76\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the unique two-digit positive integer \\( t \\) for which the last two digits of \\( 11t \\) are 36 is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{76}\n\\]",
