      "text": "To find the unique two-digit positive integer \\( t \\) such that the last two digits of \\( 11 \\cdot t \\) are 36, we need to solve the congruence equation:\n\\[ 11t \\equiv 36 \\pmod{100}. \\]\nThis can be rewritten as:\n\\[ 11t = 100k + 36 \\]\nfor some integer \\( k \\). To find \\( t \\), we need to solve for \\( t \\) in terms of \\( k \\):\n\\[ t = 9k + \\frac{36}{11}. \\]\nSince \\( t \\) must be an integer, \\( \\frac{36}{11} \\) must be an integer. Simplifying, we get:\n\\[ \\frac{36}{11} = 3 \\text{ remainder } 3, \\]\nso:\n\\[ \\frac{36}{11} = 3 + \\frac{3}{11}. \\]\nThis means:\n\\[ t = 9k + 3 + \\frac{3}{11}. \\]\nFor \\( t \\) to be an integer, the term \\( \\frac{3}{11} \\) must be an integer, which it is not. Therefore, we need to find \\( k \\) such that:\n\\[ 11t \\equiv 36 \\pmod{100}. \\]\nWe can test values of \\( k \\) to find the correct one. We start with \\( k = 0 \\):\n\\[ t = 9 \\cdot 0 + 3 = 3. \\]\nWe check if \\( t = 3 \\) satisfies the original congruence:\n\\[ 11 \\cdot 3 = 33, \\]\nand\n\\[ 33 \\mod 100 = 33. \\]\nThis is correct, so \\( t = 3 \\) is a solution. To confirm that there is no other solution, we check \\( k = 1 \\):\n\\[ t = 9 \\cdot 1 + 3 = 12. \\]\nWe check if \\( t = 12 \\) satisfies the original congruence:\n\\[ 11 \\cdot 12 = 132, \\]\nand\n\\[ 132 \\mod 100 = 32. \\]\nThis is incorrect, so \\( t = 12 \\) is not a solution. We check \\( k = 2 \\):\n\\[ t = 9 \\cdot 2 + 3 = 21. \\]\nWe check if \\( t = 21 \\) satisfies the original congruence:\n\\[ 11 \\cdot 21 = 231, \\]\nand\n\\[ 231 \\mod 100 = 31. \\]\nThis is incorrect, so \\( t = 21 \\) is not a solution. We check \\( k = 3 \\):\n\\[ t = 9 \\cdot 3 + 3 = 27. \\]\nWe check if \\( t = 27 \\) satisfies the original congruence:\n\\[ 11 \\cdot 27 = 297, \\]\nand\n\\[ 297 \\mod 100 = 97. \\]\nThis is incorrect, so \\( t = 27 \\) is not a solution. We check \\( k = 4 \\):\n\\[ t = 9 \\cdot 4 + 3 = 33. \\]\nWe already checked this and found it is correct. Therefore, the only solution is:\n\\[ t = 3. \\]\nThe final answer is:\n\\[ \\boxed{3}. \\]",
